c0add1ced261e5329047aad26350d7.png) is homogeneous of degree ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044049/assets/461a35869623099aa0b6466633af6cb58920f657ab879d9cb7ccae0a05481e75.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nA function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044049/assets/257654dafc0b541dfdd00250997596cbb35da44d4bf5b201911661459fac3617.png) is homogeneous of degree ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044049/assets/56de185c0e7e838b89aec2ec1cee1ebc90029112047f9cebc42de13a1bcf3f95.png) if it satisfies the property\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044049/assets/a1ef987a703c142bd1aeb52c5a46782e54601ecf0f3176f3ba77953b5ad3e9b1.png)\n\nSo first, we compute ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044049/assets/982da4ce20585fff559fe51384832ff7ba9ab10a7ad7ddc31831c78a3a0972e5.png) and then we simplify by writing it as ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044049/assets/8808adb23bc56b3bd8dcb3427d3db0c8043fb26fa6cdcf5cc2cf0ca0d0615c94.png) for some ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044049/assets/07e057b287d9c0cb5eec4b825014b42dfb37092d6592d225299b1096ebe08bb5.png):\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044049/assets/acead36aa36a5f642d2b5d408f390634be3d01274abfb6a4196d8d332a0f82d5.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044049/assets/b7a2044031e99d2071771a1c3d1f71d6a4fd07a57b283c081d40939525b73254.png)\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044049/assets/47070dbaf25d08b50c3f42c5f4ef9ce7d02b4e541112fa3049126f2d5cdcffed.png) is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044049/assets/bf820cdf3770e08974a14218498296ee4390accf8a18601af55422206ce41385.png)" 13194139534951 true] [17592186147180 1145 "**Method 1**\n\nWe note that\n\n- the numerator of $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $3$, and\n- the denominator of $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $1$.\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $3-1 = 2$.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nA function $f(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty) = t^nf(x,y)\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $f(tx,ty)$, and then we simplify by writing it as $t^n f(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(tx, ty) &= ((tx)(ty)^{2} + (tx^{2})(ty))/(tx + ty) \\\\\n&= (t^{3}xy^{2} + t^{3}x^{2}y)/(t(x + y)) \\\\\n&= (t^{3}(xy^{2} + x^{2}y))/(t(x + y)) \\\\\n&= (t^{2}(xy^{2} + x^{2}y))/(x + y) \\\\\n&= t^{2} \\cdot \\frac{xy^{2} + x^{2}y}{x + y} \\\\\n&= t^{2}f(x, y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty) = t^2 f(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $f(x,y)$ is $2$." 13194139534951 false] [17592186147181 1145 "A function $f(x,y,z)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty, tz) = t^nf(x,y, z)\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $f(tx,ty,tz)$, and then we simplify by writing it as $t^n f(x,y,z)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(tx, ty, tz) &= (tx)^{3} + 2(ty)^{3} + (tz)^{3} \\\\\n&= t^{3}x^{3} + 2t^{3}y^{3} + t^{3}z^{3} \\\\\n&= t^{3}(x^{3} + 2y^{3} + z^{3}) \\\\\n&= t^{3}f(x, y, z)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty,tz) = t^3 f(x,y,z)\n$$\n\nand so the function $f(x,y,z)$ is homogeneous of degree $3$." 13194139534951 false] [17592186147181 1145 "A function $f(x,y,z)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty, tz) = t^nf(x,y, z).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $f(tx,ty,tz),$ and then we simplify by writing it as $t^n f(x,y,z)$ for some $n\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty,tz) & =(tx)^{3}+2(ty)^{3}+(tz)^{3} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}{x}^{3}+2{t}^{3}{y}^{3}+{t}^{3}{z}^{3} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}({x}^{3}+2{y}^{3}+{z}^{3}) \\\\  & ={t}^{3}f(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty,tz) = t^3 f(x,y,z)\n$$\n\nand so the function $f(x,y,z)$ is homogeneous of degree $3.$" 13194139534951 true] [17592186147182 1100 "Find the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y) = \\dfrac{1}{x}\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{y}{x}\\right)$." 13194139534951 false] [17592186147182 1100 "Find the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y) = \\dfrac{1}{x}\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{y}{x}\\right).$" 13194139534951 true] [17592186147182 1145 "A function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty) = t^ng(x,y)\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $g(tx,ty)$, and then we simplify by writing it as $t^n g(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(tx, ty) &= \\frac{1}{tx}\\cos (\\frac{ty}{tx}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{tx}\\cos (\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{t} \\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\cos (\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\\n&= t^{-1} \\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\cos (\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\\n&= t^{-1}g(x, y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty) = t^{-1} g(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y)$ is $-1$." 13194139534951 false] [17592186147182 1145 "A function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty) = t^ng(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $g(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as $t^n g(x,y)$ for some $n\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(tx,ty) & =\\frac{1}{tx}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{ty}{tx}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{tx}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & ={t}^{-1}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & ={t}^{-1}g(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty) = t^{-1} g(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y)$ is $-1.$" 13194139534951 true] [17592186147183 1100 "Which of the following polynomials are homogeneous?\n\n- $f(x,y) = x^2+x+y$\n\n- $g(x,y) = x^6-2x^3y^3 + y^6$\n\n- $h(x,y) = 6$" 13194139534951 true] [17592186147183 1100 "Which of the following polynomials are homogeneous?\n\n1. $f(x,y) = x^2+x+y$\n2. $g(x,y) = x^6-2x^3y^3 + y^6$\n3. $h(x,y) = 6$" 13194139534951 false] [17592186147183 1145 "Let's go through each of the options.\n\n- The function $f(x,y)$ is not homogeneous. It is a polynomial of degree $2$, but not all the terms have the same degree. The first term has degree $2$, while the other terms have degree $1$.\n- The function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $6$. It is a polynomial of degree $6$, and all of its terms are of degree $6$.\n- The function $h(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $0$. It is a polynomial of degree $0$, and all of its terms are of degree $0$. (There is only one term, and it has degree $0$.)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only." 13194139534951 false] [17592186147183 1145 "Let's go through each of the options.\n\n- The function $f(x,y)$ is not homogeneous. It is a polynomial of degree $2,$ but not all the terms have the same degree. The first term has degree $2,$ while the other terms have degree $1.$\n\n- The function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $6$. It is a polynomial of degree $6,$ and all of its terms are of degree $6.$\n\n- The function $h(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $0$. It is a polynomial of degree $0,$ and all of its terms are of degree $0.$ (There is only one term, and it has degree $0.$)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only." 13194139534951 true] [17592186147532 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322063 13194139534951 true] [17592186147532 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322070 13194139534951 true] [17592186147532 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322133 13194139534951 true] [17592186147532 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322140 13194139534951 true] [17592186147533 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322049 13194139534951 true] [17592186147533 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322084 13194139534951 true] [17592186147533 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322105 13194139534951 true] [17592186147533 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322112 13194139534951 true] [17592186147534 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322077 13194139534951 true] [17592186147534 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322098 13194139534951 true] [17592186147534 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322147 13194139534951 true] [17592186147534 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322154 13194139534951 true] [17592186147535 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322056 13194139534951 true] [17592186147535 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322091 13194139534951 true] [17592186147535 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322119 13194139534951 true] [17592186147535 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322126 13194139534951 true] [17592186148065 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534951 true] [17592186148065 1100 "Is the function $f(x, y, z) = 3x^{2} + xy - 4z^{2}$ homogeneous? If so, what is its degree?" 13194139534951 false] [17592186148065 1100 "Is the function $f(x,y,z)=3{x}^{2}+xy-4{z}^{2}$ homogeneous? If so, what is its degree?" 13194139534951 true] [17592186148065 1145 "A function $f(x,y,z)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty,tz)={t}^{n}f(x,y,z).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $f(tx,ty,tz),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}f(x,y,z)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty,tz) & =3(tx)^{2}+(tx)(ty)-4(tz)^{2} \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}xy-4{t}^{2}{z}^{2} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}(3{x}^{2}+xy-4{z}^{2}) \\\\  & ={t}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty,tz)={t}^{2}f(x,y,z)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $f(x,y,z)$ is $2.$" 13194139534951 true] [17592186148066 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534951 true] [17592186148066 1100 "Which of the following polynomials are homogeneous?\n\n- $f(x)=2x+1$\n- $g(x,y)={x}^{3}+3xy$\n- $h(x,y)={x}^{5}-{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-{y}^{5}$" 13194139534951 true] [17592186148066 1100 "Which of the following polynomials are homogeneous?\n\n1. $f(x) = 2x + 1$\n2. $g(x, y) = x^{3} + 3xy$\n3. $h(x, y) = x^{5} - x^{2}y^{3} - y^{5}$" 13194139534951 false] [17592186148066 1145 "Let's go through each of the options.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not homogeneous. It is a polynomial of degree $1.$ However, it also contains a constant term. Therefore not all of its terms are of degree $1.$\n\n- The function $g(x,y)$ is not homogeneous. It is a polynomial of degree $3.$ However, it also contains a term ($3xy$) that is of degree $2.$ Therefore, not all of its terms are of degree $3.$\n\n- The function $h(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $5.$ It is a polynomial of degree $5,$ and all of its terms are of degree $5.$" 13194139534951 true] [17592186148073 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534951 true] [17592186148073 1100 "What is the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x, y)$ defined as\n\n$g(x, y) = \\frac{xy - x^{2}}{3y^{2}}$?" 13194139534951 false] [17592186148073 1100 "What is the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y)$ defined as\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=\\frac{xy-{x}^{2}}{3{y}^{2}}\\,?\n$$" 13194139534951 true] [17592186148073 1145 "**Method 1**\n\nWe note that:\n\n- The numerator of $g(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $2.$\n\n- The denominator of $g(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $2.$\n\nTherefore, the function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $2-2=0.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nA function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)={t}^{n}g(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $g(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}g(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(tx)(ty)-(tx)^{2}}{3(ty)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}xy-{t}^{2}{x}^{2}}{3{t}^{2}{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}(xy-{x}^{2})}{{t}^{2}(3{x}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{{t}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{xy-{x}^{2}}{(3{x}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{xy-{x}^{2}}{(3{x}^{2})} \\\\  & =g(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${t}^{0}=1,$ the equality $g(tx,ty)=g(x,y)$ is equivalent to\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)={t}^{0}g(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $0.$" 13194139534951 true] [17592186148074 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534951 true] [17592186148074 1100 "Find the degree of the homogeneous function $h(x, y) = \\frac{5y}{2x^{2} + 8xy}\\cos (\\frac{x}{y})$." 13194139534951 false] [17592186148074 1100 "Find the degree of the homogeneous function $h(x,y)=\\frac{5y}{2{x}^{2}+8xy}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}).$" 13194139534951 true] [17592186148074 1145 "A function $h(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nh(tx,ty)={t}^{n}h(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $h(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}h(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(tx,ty) & =\\frac{5(ty)}{2(tx)^{2}+8(tx)(ty)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{tx}{ty}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5ty}{2{t}^{2}{x}^{2}+8{t}^{2}xy}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{tx}{ty}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5ty}{{t}^{2}(2{x}^{2}+8xy)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{tx}{ty}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5y}{t(2{x}^{2}+8xy)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot \\frac{5y}{2{x}^{2}+8xy}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & ={t}^{-1}\\cdot \\frac{5y}{2{x}^{2}+8xy}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & ={t}^{-1}\\cdot h(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nh(tx,ty)=\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot h(x,y)={t}^{-1}h(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $h(x,y)$ is $-1.$" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322049 1017 #edb/ref 17592186322050 13194139534951 true] [17592186322049 1089 "q-64179" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322049 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534951 true] [17592186322049 1100 "Is the function $g(x,y)=2{x}^{3}+3{y}^{2}$ homogeneous? If so, what is its degree?" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322049 1113 #uuid "6ab09b8d-c62f-59d4-a051-b0634322aef9" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322049 1145 "A function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)={t}^{n}g(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $g(tx,ty),$ and then we try to simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}g(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(tx,ty) & =2(tx)^{3}+3(ty)^{2} \\\\  & =2{t}^{3}{x}^{3}+3{t}^{2}{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's impossible to write this as\n\n$$\n{t}^{n}g(x,y)={t}^{n}(2{x}^{3}+3{y}^{2})\n$$\n\nand so $g(x,y)$ is not homogeneous." 13194139534951 true] [17592186322050 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322051 13194139534951 true] [17592186322050 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322052 13194139534951 true] [17592186322050 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322053 13194139534951 true] [17592186322050 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322054 13194139534951 true] [17592186322050 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322055 13194139534951 true] [17592186322050 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534951 true] [17592186322050 1041 "selection" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322050 1104 #edb/ref 17592186322055 13194139534951 true] [17592186322050 1110 #uuid "d343bb85-be51-5ab3-9b47-5fae731c4cf9" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322051 1034 "Homogeneous of degree $5$" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322051 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534951 true] [17592186322051 1111 #uuid "ec728116-cf65-5473-b63d-21c3e13afe63" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322052 1034 "Homogeneous of degree $2$" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322052 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534951 true] [17592186322052 1111 #uuid "24dac729-c4a2-5b85-b9fa-806eb0bcd744" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322053 1034 "Homogeneous of degree $6$" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322053 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534951 true] [17592186322053 1111 #uuid "f740ae6a-ed59-5897-bbf6-f3c25555cb9d" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322054 1034 "Homogeneous of degree $3$" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322054 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534951 true] [17592186322054 1111 #uuid "3551b1b7-6fd6-599a-a7a7-4a245ee8ca5e" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322055 1034 "Not homogeneous" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322055 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534951 true] [17592186322055 1111 #uuid "f41f2221-c162-5699-9560-0cccb211c546" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322056 1017 #edb/ref 17592186322057 13194139534951 true] [17592186322056 1089 "q-64198" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322056 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534951 true] [17592186322056 1100 "Which of the following polynomials are homogeneous?\n\n- $f(y)=5{y}^{2}$\n- $g(x,y)=2{x}^{3}y+x{y}^{4}$\n- $h(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}-xyz$" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322056 1113 #uuid "a1f59ac7-fe59-5414-84e5-39e2cb13a6a8" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322056 1145 "Let's go through each of the options.\n\n- The function $f(y)$ is homogeneous of degree $2.$ It is a polynomial of degree $2,$ with no linear or constant term.\n\n- The function $g(x,y)$ is not homogeneous. It is a polynomial of degree $5.$ However, it also contains a term ($2{x}^{3}y$) that is of degree $4.$ Therefore, not all of its terms are of degree $5.$\n\n- The function $h(x,y,z)$ is homogeneous of degree $3.$ It is a polynomial of degree $3,$ and all of its terms are of degree $3.$" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322057 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322058 13194139534951 true] [17592186322057 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322059 13194139534951 true] [17592186322057 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322060 13194139534951 true] [17592186322057 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322061 13194139534951 true] [17592186322057 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322062 13194139534951 true] [17592186322057 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534951 true] [17592186322057 1041 "selection" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322057 1104 #edb/ref 17592186322059 13194139534951 true] [17592186322057 1110 #uuid "c4505c20-b837-51a5-b06d-f2bf8603d0f6" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322058 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322058 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534951 true] [17592186322058 1111 #uuid "dd60a862-75cb-5583-ba7e-d15ca3d53395" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322059 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322059 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534951 true] [17592186322059 1111 #uuid "80df5bf2-82a3-5520-b547-b636048cca37" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322060 1034 "I only" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322060 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534951 true] [17592186322060 1111 #uuid "ae28ebe7-48ee-5c37-ad70-347e63720d30" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322061 1034 "III only" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322061 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534951 true] [17592186322061 1111 #uuid "8f77516d-4cde-561b-9cc5-d71367490fe4" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322062 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322062 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534951 true] [17592186322062 1111 #uuid "9c761827-1e1e-5344-8247-75bdf7dbeda6" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322063 1017 #edb/ref 17592186322064 13194139534951 true] [17592186322063 1089 "q-64200" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322063 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534951 true] [17592186322063 1100 "What is the degree of the homogeneous function $h(x,y)$ defined as\n\n$$\nh(x,y)=\\frac{x{y}^{3}+4{y}^{4}}{2{y}^{3}}\\,?\n$$" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322063 1113 #uuid "3ac6de04-c052-57c1-969b-cc05e34d199a" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322063 1145 "**Method 1**\n\nWe note that:\n\n- The numerator of $h(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $4.$\n\n- The denominator of $h(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $3.$\n\nTherefore, the function $h(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $4-3=1.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nA function $h(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nh(tx,ty)={t}^{n}h(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $h(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}h(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(tx)(ty)^{3}+4(ty)^{4}}{2(ty)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}x{y}^{3}+4{t}^{4}{y}^{4}}{2{t}^{3}{y}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}(x{y}^{3}+4{y}^{4})}{{t}^{3}(2{y}^{3})} \\\\  & =t\\cdot \\frac{x{y}^{3}+4{y}^{4}}{2{y}^{3}} \\\\  & =t\\cdot \\frac{x{y}^{3}+4{y}^{4}}{2{y}^{3}} \\\\  & =th(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nh(tx,ty)=th(x,y)={t}^{1}h(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the function $h(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $1.$" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322064 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322065 13194139534951 true] [17592186322064 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322066 13194139534951 true] [17592186322064 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322067 13194139534951 true] [17592186322064 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322068 13194139534951 true] [17592186322064 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322069 13194139534951 true] [17592186322064 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534951 true] [17592186322064 1041 "selection" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322064 1104 #edb/ref 17592186322067 13194139534951 true] [17592186322064 1110 #uuid "b5dfa529-3cec-5997-ba2d-58f2ae12b76c" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322065 1034 "3" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322065 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534951 true] [17592186322065 1111 #uuid "c1ef4c83-bb89-5f50-9623-e35ef6c81ea7" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322066 1034 "7" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322066 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534951 true] [17592186322066 1111 #uuid "890a71b1-a389-5054-903c-8cd38b043e01" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322067 1034 "1" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322067 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534951 true] [17592186322067 1111 #uuid "d5bc7514-f20f-5675-bc4a-5a417d736d4d" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322068 1034 "4" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322068 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534951 true] [17592186322068 1111 #uuid "c50aedc9-8afa-5edf-b10b-190e9366624a" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322069 1034 "-1" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322069 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534951 true] [17592186322069 1111 #uuid "0f492ce2-7d59-504d-98f8-6a050c730d87" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322070 1017 #edb/ref 17592186322071 13194139534951 true] [17592186322070 1089 "q-64201" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322070 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534951 true] [17592186322070 1100 "What is the degree of the homogeneous function $f(x,y)$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{5{y}^{2}+xy}{2{x}^{4}}\\,?\n$$" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322070 1113 #uuid "9ef1e233-ce29-56eb-9a97-4a5290698d4a" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322070 1145 "**Method 1**\n\nWe note that:\n\n- The numerator of $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $2.$\n\n- The denominator of $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $4.$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $2-4=-2.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nA function $f(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)={t}^{n}f(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $f(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}f(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{5(ty)^{2}+(tx)(ty)}{2(tx)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5{t}^{2}{y}^{2}+{t}^{2}xy}{2{t}^{4}{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}(5{y}^{2}+xy)}{{t}^{4}(2{x}^{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5{y}^{2}+xy}{{t}^{2}(2{x}^{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{5{y}^{2}+xy}{2{x}^{4}} \\\\  & ={t}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{5{y}^{2}+xy}{2{x}^{4}} \\\\  & ={t}^{-2}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)={t}^{-2}f(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the function $f(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $-2.$" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322071 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322072 13194139534951 true] [17592186322071 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322073 13194139534951 true] [17592186322071 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322074 13194139534951 true] [17592186322071 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322075 13194139534951 true] [17592186322071 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322076 13194139534951 true] [17592186322071 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534951 true] [17592186322071 1041 "selection" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322071 1104 #edb/ref 17592186322076 13194139534951 true] [17592186322071 1110 #uuid "b51e107b-3623-5a0e-bd0c-f28a2f6cd016" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322072 1034 "3" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322072 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534951 true] [17592186322072 1111 #uuid "4f0fcf48-5fec-5e42-b451-6fbd966fa808" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322073 1034 "2" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322073 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534951 true] [17592186322073 1111 #uuid "ab59215e-1a30-5f99-a031-ac19926709ef" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322074 1034 "1" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322074 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534951 true] [17592186322074 1111 #uuid "cfb1eb35-5d95-5d73-97a8-fc216d19c8a1" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322075 1034 "-4" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322075 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534951 true] [17592186322075 1111 #uuid "b26f55e1-684c-57b7-a33e-8038bf020c82" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322076 1034 "-2" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322076 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534951 true] [17592186322076 1111 #uuid "27d2992d-f8eb-5cca-9bf7-df6878c0e595" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322077 1017 #edb/ref 17592186322078 13194139534951 true] [17592186322077 1089 "q-64202" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322077 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534951 true] [17592186322077 1100 "Find the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y)=\\frac{y}{3x-y}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}).$" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322077 1113 #uuid "a46a6a02-6826-5c1a-9f58-69b0f1d3511a" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322077 1145 "A function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)={t}^{n}g(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $g(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}g(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(tx,ty) & =\\frac{ty}{3tx-ty}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{tx}{ty}) \\\\  & =\\frac{ty}{t(3x-y)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{tx}{ty}) \\\\  & =\\frac{t}{t}\\cdot \\frac{y}{3x-y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{3x-y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =g(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${t}^{0}=1,$ the equality $g(tx,ty)=g(x,y)$ is equivalent to\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)=1\\cdot g(x,y)={t}^{0}g(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y)$ is $0.$" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322078 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322079 13194139534951 true] [17592186322078 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322080 13194139534951 true] [17592186322078 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322081 13194139534951 true] [17592186322078 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322082 13194139534951 true] [17592186322078 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322083 13194139534951 true] [17592186322078 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534951 true] [17592186322078 1041 "selection" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322078 1104 #edb/ref 17592186322081 13194139534951 true] [17592186322078 1110 #uuid "4d851aee-96bf-5565-a690-707722840eb9" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322079 1034 "2" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322079 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534951 true] [17592186322079 1111 #uuid "ae178ad1-b94f-5ffc-90a2-a3384524cfa2" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322080 1034 "1" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322080 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534951 true] [17592186322080 1111 #uuid "7879a9be-6f89-5f1b-a6e8-335e5bc09b9b" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322081 1034 "0" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322081 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534951 true] [17592186322081 1111 #uuid "dfdb67e6-ad34-5b59-b888-d004e24d62ec" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322082 1034 "-1" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322082 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534951 true] [17592186322082 1111 #uuid "17954489-6666-5652-9291-67582548e6ff" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322083 1034 "-2" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322083 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534951 true] [17592186322083 1111 #uuid "433d2eef-57f7-5a4f-acec-d5fafbd80e50" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322084 1017 #edb/ref 17592186322085 13194139534951 true] [17592186322084 1089 "q-79577" 13194139534951 true] [17592186