nly" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322129 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534951 true] [17592186322129 1111 #uuid "55fb6df6-0f8d-546b-831d-4aea587f33c5" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322130 1034 "III only" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322130 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534951 true] [17592186322130 1111 #uuid "cb33fa20-a90e-5e8d-a85b-c3373dabae8f" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322131 1034 "None" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322131 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534951 true] [17592186322131 1111 #uuid "2d9167b0-29e3-5817-87c8-fce4fdfa391d" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322132 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322132 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534951 true] [17592186322132 1111 #uuid "8581da7a-a4b3-5ebd-9040-6415f148e75e" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322133 1017 #edb/ref 17592186322134 13194139534951 true] [17592186322133 1089 "q-90588" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322133 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534951 true] [17592186322133 1100 "What is the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y)$ defined as\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2xy-6{y}^{2}}\\,?\n$$" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322133 1113 #uuid "431e940d-dc2f-5be2-ab99-48a93496e073" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322133 1145 "**Method 1**\n\nWe note that:\n\n- The numerator of $g(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $4.$\n\n- The denominator of $g(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $2.$\n\nTherefore, the function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $4-2=2.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nA function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)={t}^{n}g(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $g(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}g(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(tx)^{4}}{2(tx)(ty)-6(ty)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}{x}^{4}}{2{t}^{2}xy-6{t}^{2}{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}({x}^{4})}{{t}^{2}(2xy-6{y}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}}{{t}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{4}}{2xy-6{y}^{2}} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{4}}{2xy-6{y}^{2}} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}g(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)={t}^{2}g(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $2.$" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322134 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322135 13194139534951 true] [17592186322134 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322136 13194139534951 true] [17592186322134 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322137 13194139534951 true] [17592186322134 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322138 13194139534951 true] [17592186322134 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322139 13194139534951 true] [17592186322134 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534951 true] [17592186322134 1041 "selection" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322134 1104 #edb/ref 17592186322136 13194139534951 true] [17592186322134 1110 #uuid "0aacdfd2-6bef-52b8-939d-7623e47756c8" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322135 1034 "3" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322135 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534951 true] [17592186322135 1111 #uuid "953c2e68-8113-5a93-9006-d80e85e73567" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322136 1034 "2" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322136 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534951 true] [17592186322136 1111 #uuid "b161eb63-e9cb-5ead-ae51-3c26a227a501" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322137 1034 "4" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322137 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534951 true] [17592186322137 1111 #uuid "88e193d0-8014-5880-bf08-dfcc62ef0807" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322138 1034 "-1" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322138 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534951 true] [17592186322138 1111 #uuid "08efc616-98d7-5c4f-b6d3-12a134d11a55" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322139 1034 "-2" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322139 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534951 true] [17592186322139 1111 #uuid "8e5c06d9-e4ef-50aa-9f5b-99f04937f098" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322140 1017 #edb/ref 17592186322141 13194139534951 true] [17592186322140 1089 "q-90589" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322140 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534951 true] [17592186322140 1100 "What is the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y)$ defined as\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=\\frac{x{y}^{3}}{4y-x}\\,?\n$$" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322140 1113 #uuid "28bf47fa-7b88-58b0-b97d-7e859e594577" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322140 1145 "**Method 1**\n\nWe note that:\n\n- The numerator of $g(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $4.$\n\n- The denominator of $g(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $1.$\n\nTherefore, the function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $4-1=3.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nA function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)={t}^{n}g(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $g(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}g(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(tx)(ty)^{3}}{4(ty)-(tx)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}x{y}^{3}}{4ty-tx} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}(x{y}^{3})}{t(4y-x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}}{t}\\cdot \\frac{x{y}^{3}}{4y-x} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{x{y}^{3}}{4y-x} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}g(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)={t}^{3}g(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $3.$" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322141 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322142 13194139534951 true] [17592186322141 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322143 13194139534951 true] [17592186322141 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322144 13194139534951 true] [17592186322141 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322145 13194139534951 true] [17592186322141 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322146 13194139534951 true] [17592186322141 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534951 true] [17592186322141 1041 "selection" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322141 1104 #edb/ref 17592186322142 13194139534951 true] [17592186322141 1110 #uuid "41a3ff59-8cc6-5027-966f-2e310f50916f" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322142 1034 "3" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322142 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534951 true] [17592186322142 1111 #uuid "1d047bd4-2804-5336-aaba-c10002d15c11" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322143 1034 "4" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322143 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534951 true] [17592186322143 1111 #uuid "bd0f2413-dbc0-577d-a215-c975da5ac20f" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322144 1034 "0" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322144 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534951 true] [17592186322144 1111 #uuid "4216c417-13f1-53ba-a647-1ba8ed35faa3" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322145 1034 "-1" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322145 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534951 true] [17592186322145 1111 #uuid "26220314-b114-5eca-b0c5-f8051d2a8451" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322146 1034 "-2" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322146 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534951 true] [17592186322146 1111 #uuid "69eb8e09-6283-5c5c-9550-fd5b7919230c" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322147 1017 #edb/ref 17592186322148 13194139534951 true] [17592186322147 1089 "q-90594" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322147 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534951 true] [17592186322147 1100 "Find the degree of the homogeneous function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x+3y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y}).$" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322147 1113 #uuid "f0b33e13-a7b4-5f66-b92e-9cd183c50e94" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322147 1145 "A function $f(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)={t}^{n}f(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $f(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}f(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{tx+3ty}{(tx)^{2}(ty)^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{tx}{2ty}) \\\\  & =\\frac{t(x+3y)}{{t}^{4}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{t}{{t}^{4}}\\cdot \\frac{x+3y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{x+3y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y}) \\\\  & ={t}^{-3}\\cdot \\frac{x+3y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y}) \\\\  & ={t}^{-3}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)={t}^{-3}f(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $f(x,y)$ is $-3.$" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322148 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322149 13194139534951 true] [17592186322148 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322150 13194139534951 true] [17592186322148 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322151 13194139534951 true] [17592186322148 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322152 13194139534951 true] [17592186322148 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322153 13194139534951 true] [17592186322148 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534951 true] [17592186322148 1041 "selection" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322148 1104 #edb/ref 17592186322152 13194139534951 true] [17592186322148 1110 #uuid "e26b0697-9c74-532b-9d37-bb7892b296f3" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322149 1034 "3" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322149 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534951 true] [17592186322149 1111 #uuid "096cb871-9cd2-5381-b1d6-302d1d8a49b8" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322150 1034 "1" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322150 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534951 true] [17592186322150 1111 #uuid "d0d8e547-67e0-5341-bcc3-0b33d601d366" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322151 1034 "-4" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322151 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534951 true] [17592186322151 1111 #uuid "01f568b5-4c09-551f-8de5-471218c5e535" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322152 1034 "-3" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322152 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534951 true] [17592186322152 1111 #uuid "5e93f0a0-4ccf-5a85-a46d-63601d7f7f30" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322153 1034 "-2" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322153 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534951 true] [17592186322153 1111 #uuid "55dbf86a-4da3-5e8c-8f2d-027af549b1b1" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322154 1017 #edb/ref 17592186322155 13194139534951 true] [17592186322154 1089 "q-90596" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322154 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534951 true] [17592186322154 1100 "Find the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y)=\\frac{4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3y}{x}).$" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322154 1113 #uuid "01a90fbd-9569-5668-b55c-c57b9ff2d6b3" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322154 1145 "A function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)={t}^{n}g(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $g(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}g(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(tx,ty) & =\\frac{4(tx)^{2}-(ty)^{2}}{(tx)}\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3ty}{tx}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4{t}^{2}{x}^{2}-{t}^{2}{y}^{2}}{tx}\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3ty}{tx}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}(4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})}{tx}\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{t}\\cdot \\frac{4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3y}{x}) \\\\  & =t\\cdot \\frac{4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3y}{x}) \\\\  & =tg(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)=tg(x,y)={t}^{1}g(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y)$ is $1.$" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322155 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322156 13194139534951 true] [17592186322155 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322157 13194139534951 true] [17592186322155 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322158 13194139534951 true] [17592186322155 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322159 13194139534951 true] [17592186322155 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322160 13194139534951 true] [17592186322155 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534951 true] [17592186322155 1041 "selection" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322155 1104 #edb/ref 17592186322157 13194139534951 true] [17592186322155 1110 #uuid "82a01898-c253-5883-a712-a2c5639abd26" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322156 1034 "3" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322156 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534951 true] [17592186322156 1111 #uuid "f2271484-8110-5406-8f65-2e5682e1dc94" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322157 1034 "1" 13194139534951 true] [17592186322157 11